<details class="methodology">
  <summary>Methodology</summary>

  <h4>1. Data</h4>
  <p>Four sources feed the model. (i) Provincial voting-intention polls from 2014 to the present,
  compiled from the public tables of Wikipedia (CC BY-SA), each entry linking to the polling firm's
  original release; regional breakdowns (Montreal, Quebec City, rest of Quebec) come from the firms'
  reports when published. (ii) The official results of the 2014, 2018, 2022 and 2026 general elections
  (Élections Québec), expressed on the 2026 electoral map (127 ridings), earlier elections through a
  geographic correspondence of polling divisions, as well as the by-elections held since the 2026
  general election (DGEQ). (iii) The 2021 census (Statistics Canada), aggregated from dissemination areas
  to ridings. (iv) The status of incumbent members, retirements and leaders' candidacies, collected from
  the pages of the National Assembly.</p>

  <h4>2. Compositional representation</h4>
  <p>A vector of voting intentions is a composition: its shares are positive and sum to one, so the
  analysis is carried out in the simplex rather than in the Euclidean space of percentages. All
  compositional responses and predictors are transformed into isometric log-ratio (ILR) coordinates, an
  orthonormal basis of dimension k−1 in which linear operations and distances are valid. Multiplicative
  adjustments (local premiums and penalties) are applied as compositional perturbations, the internal
  operation of the simplex. Parties not broken out in older polls are handled by amalgamation into the
  residual share, with multiplicative replacement of zeros.</p>

  <h4>3. National trend</h4>
  <p>The trajectory of voting intentions is estimated by Gaussian process regression, one independent
  process per ILR coordinate, Matérn kernel (ν = 3/2). The observation variance of each poll is computed
  per coordinate: delta method applied to multinomial sampling, Var(ilr_c) = Σ_p M²_cp / (n x_p), where M
  is the Jacobian of the ILR transformation. A multiplicative scale per coordinate, estimated on a grid,
  absorbs overdispersion between polling firms. The hyperparameters (range, noise scale and
  non-stationarity parameters) are marginalised: the prediction is the average of the grid models
  weighted by their marginal likelihood, so that hyperparameter uncertainty is included in the
  predictive intervals.</p>
  <p>Two forms of non-stationarity are allowed, each containing the stationary model as a special case so
  that selection rests on the likelihood rather than on a discretionary choice. First, breakpoints at
  leadership changes (January 14 and April 12, 2026): the covariance between regimes is multiplied by
  ρ<sup>|Δr|</sup>, ρ ∈ [0, 1] estimated by marginal likelihood, ρ = 1 restoring the stationary kernel; a
  partial break is retained on one coordinate, the stationary model elsewhere. Second, a dilation of the
  time axis during campaigns (from the writ to election day, 2014, 2018, 2022 and 2026 windows): one
  campaign day counts as k days of kernel time, k shared across all campaigns (hence identified mainly by
  past campaigns) and k = 1 restoring the uniform clock. The retained dilation is strong: effective range
  of about 40 to 190 days during campaigns depending on the coordinate, against 300 to 1500 outside
  campaigns. The calibration of the predictive intervals is measured by sequential validation: at each
  monthly cutoff from 2016 to 2026, the model fitted on earlier polls only predicts the next poll. The
  nominal 90% interval covers 79% of these 675 predictions (95% during campaigns, 78% outside). Random
  k-fold cross-validation gives 90.5%, a figure that measures interpolation of the series, not
  prediction.</p>

  <h4>4. Regional polls</h4>
  <p>The regional breakdowns published by polling firms (Montreal metropolitan area, Quebec City
  metropolitan area, rest of Quebec), all firms, released since the previous election are pooled by
  region as a weighted mean. The weight of a poll is its regional sample size multiplied by
  exp(−age/τ), age being measured on the clock of the national trend (section 3): one campaign day
  counts as D days. τ = 240 days and D = 16 are estimated by one-step-ahead forecasting of the
  region-province gap of each of the 152 regionally broken-down polls published from 2018 to 2026, using
  only earlier polls of the same cycle, on grids containing no decay and no dilation. The effective
  constant is thus 15 days during campaigns and 240 days outside; the squared forecast error is 0.163,
  against 0.216 for a fixed 14-day window and 0.262 without regional polls. For each region, the gap
  between the region and the province in these polls, in centred log-ratios of the five main parties,
  minus the same gap in the results of the previous election, shifts the ridings of the region,
  multiplied by a coefficient estimated jointly with that of the mother-tongue swing (section 5) on the
  2014→2018, 2018→2022 and 2022→2026 transitions: 0.75 for regions, 0.25 for language. With both
  coefficients chosen on two transitions and evaluated on the third, at known provincial vote, the root
  mean squared error of riding shares falls from 5.66 to 5.44 points and the number of correct winners
  rises from 309 to 316 out of 348 ridings evaluated. On the eve of the three elections, national trend
  included, the absolute seat error of the central riding-level scenario falls from 36 to 30 over the
  three combined.</p>

  <h4>5. Riding model</h4>
  <p>The starting point of each riding is its result at the general election of October 5, 2026. No
  differential-turnout correction is applied: the shrinkage weight of that correction, estimated on a
  grid against the final polls of 2022 and 2026 combined, is zero (mean absolute error of 1.89 points
  without correction, 2.06 with). The estimated provincial swing (section 3) is applied in log-ratios.
  Four estimated adjustments are layered on top, all as compositional perturbations:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Incumbency premium: a seat whose member elected in 2026 does not run again or now sits under
    another banner loses the personal premium, estimated by comparing incumbent and new candidates from
    2018 to 2022 (factor 1.125 on the holding party's share). Evaluated on the 2014→2018, 2018→2022 and
    2022→2026 transitions, each predicted from the other two (incumbents identified by matching the names
    of members and candidates), the chosen penalty corresponds to a factor of 1.11 to 1.16 and reduces
    the eve absolute seat error, national trend included, from 32 to 26 over the three elections.</li>
    <li>Leader effect: estimated on 37 leader candidacies at general elections from 1976 to 2026,
    classified as arrival (leader in a riding that is not their own, 8 cases), continuation (same leader,
    same riding, 14 cases) or departure (riding of the previous leader without them, 15 cases). Each case
    is the log gap between the share obtained and the share predicted by uniform provincial swing. A
    constant mean per type and a Gaussian process over the year are compared in sequential forecasting
    (each election predicted from earlier elections only); the constant mean prevails for all three
    types: arrival ×1.39, continuation ×1.14, departure ×0.90.</li>
    <li>Mother-tongue swing: the francophone and non-francophone breakdowns of the two latest Léger polls
    give, for each group, the log-ratio change in its voting intentions since the end of the previous
    campaign (mean weighted by sample sizes). Each riding receives the mixture of these changes according
    to its language composition (2021 census, mother tongue, aggregated from dissemination areas),
    centred on the mean of the ridings, multiplied by a coefficient of 0.25 estimated jointly with that
    of the regional polls (section 4). On its own, this swing reduces the root mean squared error of
    riding shares on each of the three transitions predicted from the other two at known provincial vote:
    from 5.51 to 5.39, from 7.43 to 7.14 and from 5.23 to 4.83 points. The same construction by age group
    (18-34, 35-54, 55 and over) brings no gain: level differences between groups are marked, but their
    changes from one election to the next are similar and the age composition varies little from one
    riding to another.</li>
    <li>By-elections: each by-election held since the 2026 general election provides a direct local
    measurement. The gap between its result and the contemporaneous provincial swing, net of the
    withdrawn incumbency premium, enters the local deviation with a weight of 0.5. This weight is a
    documented prior and not an estimated parameter: no historical series of by-elections is available to
    fit it, and these contests differ from general elections in turnout and context. A sensitivity
    analysis of the weight is provided with the code.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>No local deviation is predicted from the demographic profile. A multivariate Gaussian process
  regression (Matérn kernel, one process per ILR coordinate) of each riding's change on its census profile
  (language, education, immigration, employment structure, age, income, density) and on its previous
  deviation reaches an R² of 0.44 within a transition, but −0.16 from one transition to the next (training
  on 2018→2022, predicting 2022→2026). Evaluated in seats on the three transitions 2014→2018, 2018→2022 and
  2022→2026, each predicted from the other two, it accumulates 221 seats of error against 40 for carrying
  results forward without local deviation; on 2026, at known provincial vote, it raises the root mean
  squared error of riding shares from 4.95 to 5.92 points.</p>
  <p>The regional (section 4) and mother-tongue adjustments bear on the gap between the polls published
  since the 2026 election and the results of that election; they apply as soon as regional or language
  breakdowns are published after election day. The displayed forecast includes the adjustments for
  which such breakdowns exist on the date of computation.</p>

  <h4>6. Simulation and uncertainty quantification</h4>
  <p>The seat distribution is obtained by Monte Carlo simulation (5,000 draws) propagating four sources
  of uncertainty: (i) the posterior distribution of the national trend; (ii) a systemic shock common to
  all ridings (collective bias of polling firms and drift of opinion until election day), calibrated on
  the gap between the last polls and the actual results of 2018 and 2022, isotropic in ILR; (iii) a
  regional shock, local deviations being spatially correlated; (iv) the idiosyncratic deviation of each
  riding, resampled from the errors of the riding model (provincial swing and mother-tongue swing) in
  previous general transitions, 2014→2018, 2018→2022 and 2022→2026 pooled (whole ILR vectors, net of their
  regional mean). These errors are scaled party by party in centred log-ratios: CAQ ×0.75, QLP ×0.75,
  CPQ ×0.5, PQ ×1, QS ×0.75. The factors are chosen on a grid containing 1 by maximising the out-of-sample
  log-score of win probabilities, the errors of each transition predicting the others (log-score from
  0.824 to 0.772). Evaluated on the three transitions, each predicted from the other two at known
  provincial vote, this scaling reduces the total log-score from 0.889 to 0.809 (riding model without
  regional polls) and improves each of the three. Each component is then bounded at ±0.75 standard
  deviation of its part in the pool. This threshold is chosen on a grid containing no bound, evaluating
  each election from the other two, on the eve and national trend included: it is retained in two of the
  three cases (1 in the third), and reduces the total log-score from 0.964 to 0.915, improving it on each
  of the three elections. Each draw assigns a winner per riding; all 127 seats are thus allocated in
  every draw.</p>
  <p>Two statistics summarise the joint distribution. The win probability of a riding is the frequency of
  wins over the draws; by linearity of expectation, its sum over ridings equals the party's expected seat
  count, and these expectations sum exactly to 127, the only additive riding-level decomposition. The
  reported central scenario is the medoid of the joint distribution: the draw minimising the mean L1
  distance to the other draws. Unlike the vector of marginal medians, which matches no attainable
  scenario and does not sum to the number of seats, the medoid is an actual realisation, consistent
  across parties. The reported voting intentions describe the state of opinion on the date of
  computation; the seat simulation bears on election day, set by law on October 7, 2030. Since each draw
  carries both a national vote composition and a seat allocation, the distortion of the electoral system
  is itself a joint distribution: the votes-seats panel reports the expected shares of both per party and
  the Gallagher least-squares index, √(½Σ(v−s)²), summarised as a median and 90% interval over the
  draws.</p>
  <p>Each draw is assigned to a single government scenario: majority when the first party reaches 64
  seats, minority otherwise. For a minority government, the parties whose seats, added to those of the
  first party, reach a majority are listed, excluding the Official Opposition (second party in seats): these parties hold the balance of power. In the certainty chart, each riding is assigned to its favourite party and classed by that party's probability of winning: safe (95% or more), likely (75 to 95%), lean (60 to 75%), toss-up (under 60%).</p>

  <h4>7. Validation and limitations</h4>
  <p>The out-of-sample validations of sections 4 to 6 bear on the three transitions 2014→2018, 2018→2022
  and 2022→2026, each evaluated with parameters estimated without it. The forecast published on the eve
  of the October 5, 2026 election identified the winner in 104 of the 127 ridings (106 according to the
  favourite by win probability; mean multiparty Brier score of 0.264). The absolute seat error of its
  median scenario is 28 (PQ 49 against 59 elected, CAQ 11 against 0, QS 5 against 9, CPQ 22 against 19,
  QLP 40 against 40); four of the five per-party 90% seat intervals contain the result, the CAQ's (2 to
  32) being the exception. The systemic shock is calibrated on the 2018 and 2022 elections; its isotropy
  in ILR coordinates was tested against an anisotropic alternative (principal axis of observed drifts,
  directional share on a grid) and retained by leave-one-out validation on the three available drifts.
  Differential turnout by age is not modelled (optimal weight of zero on the final polls of 2022 and 2026
  combined). Also not modelled: the individual name recognition of candidates (tested through Wikipedia
  notability, with no out-of-sample gain) and the covariance between parties in the trend. The latter was
  evaluated twice: by injecting the empirical correlation of poll residuals (rejected, the effect on seats
  remaining within simulation noise), then by intrinsic coregionalisation, the coupling matrix B being
  estimated jointly by marginal likelihood on the stacked ILR coordinates; the in-sample likelihood gain
  (+7 nats over the independent model) does not survive cross-validation (predictive log-likelihood
  lower by 25 nats over 1,355 observations, coverage unchanged), the signature of overfitting of the
  coupling hyperparameters. House effects, evaluated by temporal cross-validation, bring no predictive
  gain and are not retained. The weight of the by-election signal remains a prior that cannot be
  validated on historical data.</p>

  <h4>8. Implementation and reproducibility</h4>
  <p>All estimations (compositional transformations, Gaussian process regressions, hyperparameter
  selection and cross-validation) rely on the <a href="https://suite.tangent.to">tangent</a> computing
  suite. The complete pipeline is released under the GPL-3 licence and reproducible from the
  <a href="https://github.com/votoscope/quebec">github.com/votoscope/quebec</a> repository, the data from
  <a href="https://github.com/votoscope/data">github.com/votoscope/data</a>. The forecast is fully
  recomputed with each newly detected poll.</p>
</details>
